### What it does
The `escape` function in the `globset` crate takes a `&str` and returns a `String` where every glob meta-character is "bracketed" — i.e., wrapped in `[…]` — so the result is a valid glob pattern that matches the input literally.

### The meta-characters it escapes
| Input char | Output |
|---|---|
| `?` | `[?]` |
| `*` | `[*]` |
| `[` | `[[]` |
| `]` | `[]]` |
| `{` | `[{]` |
| `}` | `[}]` |

> Note: `!` is **not** escaped because it is only special *inside* brackets, not at the top level.

### Relevant code (from `crates/globset/src/lib.rs`, ~line 1022–1054)
```rust
/// Escape meta-characters within the given glob pattern.
///
/// The escaping works by surrounding meta-characters with brackets. For
/// example, `*` becomes `[*]`.
pub fn escape(s: &str) -> String {
    let mut escaped = String::with_capacity(s.len());
    for c in s.chars() {
        match c {
            '?' | '*' | '[' | ']' | '{' | '}' => {
                escaped.push('[');
                escaped.push(c);
                escaped.push(']');
            }
            c => {
                escaped.push(c);
            }
        }
    }
    escaped
}
```

### How it answers the task
